<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Margaret of Austria, Duchess of Savoy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1480">1480</a>–<a href="/years/1530">1530</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Margaret of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> was a daughter of Maximilian I and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-of-burgundy">Mary of Burgundy</a> who governed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> from 1507 to 1515 and from 1519 to 1530 for her nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>. Her court at Mechelen was a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> art, music and learning. In 1529 she negotiated the Treaty of Cambrai, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-cambrai">Ladies' Peace</a>, with Louise of Savoy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/margaret-of-austria-duchess-of-savoy.jpg" alt="Margaret of Austria, Duchess of Savoy" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernard van Orley · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Archduchess_Margaret_of_Austria,_Duchess_of_Savoy_(1480%E2%80%931530),_in_widow%27s_dress,_by_Bernard_van_Orley.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
